---
/** Track validity guide — how GlideComp checks that a submitted tracklog is
 * plausibly the pilot's flight of that task, which checks withhold a track
 * from scoring, and how a scorekeeper overrules the automatic verdict.
 * Sibling of the GAP, open-distance and data-cleaning pages.
 *
 * The thresholds quoted here are the constants in engine track-quality.ts —
 * keep them in step. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
import SpecRef from "../../components/SpecRef.astro";

const S7A_PDF =
  "https://www.fai.org/sites/default/files/civl/documents/sporting_code_s7_a_-_cross_country_2025.pdf";
---

<Base
  title="GlideComp - How Track Validity Checks Work"
  description="How GlideComp checks that a submitted tracklog is really that pilot's flight of that task — the day and location checks that withhold a track from scoring, the softer flags that never change a score, and the scorekeeper's power to overrule."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground p-6">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Track Validity Checks Work
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Making sure a tracklog is really your flight of this task — and saying so out loud
          when it isn't
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><a href="#why" class="hover:underline">Why Tracks Get Checked</a></li>
          <li><a href="#withheld" class="hover:underline">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</a></li>
          <li><a href="#flags" class="hover:underline">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#score" class="hover:underline">What It Does to the Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#override" class="hover:underline">The Scorekeeper Decides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#calibration" class="hover:underline">How the Thresholds Were Chosen</a></li>
          <li><a href="#disclosure" class="hover:underline">Full Disclosure on the Score Page</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* Why */}
      <section id="why" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why Tracks Get Checked</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Occasionally a tracklog reaches a task that has nothing to do with it — a flight
            from a different day, from a different site, or the wrong file picked out of a
            folder full of them. It is an easy mistake to make and a hard one to spot: the
            file is a perfectly good IGC, it opens, it parses, and it looks like flying.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Scoring cannot notice on its own, and that is deliberate. Tasks routinely run
            across UTC midnight, so GlideComp resolves a task's start gates and deadline
            <em>relative to the flight it is scoring</em> rather than to a fixed calendar day.
            That is what makes an Australian afternoon task score correctly — and it also
            means a tracklog from another day quietly carries the whole task onto its own day
            and scores as though nothing were wrong.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The Sporting Code puts the job on the software rather than on the scorer.
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">FAI Section 7A §4.4.2</a>
            requires verification that all the points used to verify a flight
            "occurred at reasonable times (e.g. on the day in question)". These checks are how
            GlideComp meets that.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Withheld */}
      <section id="withheld" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Two checks answer one question — <strong class="text-foreground">is this file this
            flight at all?</strong> Both are decided with margins so wide that no honest entry
            can reach them.
          </p>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong day</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              The track's fixes are compared against the task's own calendar day
              <em>in the competition's time zone</em>, widened by four hours at each end. A
              flight only fails if <strong class="text-foreground">every single fix</strong>
              falls outside that window — so a recorder switched on the evening before, a dawn
              launch, or a flight that crosses local midnight all pass comfortably.
            </p>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-2">
              Even then, being outside by up to a day is only flagged, not withheld: a
              recorder with its date set one day out is a real and recurring fault, and the
              flight itself is usually genuine. Only a track more than a day adrift is treated
              as a different flight.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong location</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              If no fix in the track comes within <strong class="text-foreground">100 km</strong>
              of <em>any</em> of the task's turnpoints, it is not a flight of this task. The
              distance is measured to the nearest turnpoint at the track's closest approach, so
              a long course does not make the test stricter — turnpoints are spread along it,
              and anyone who actually launched starts within metres of one.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A track that fails either check is kept out of the scoring calculation and out of
            the field analysis, where its air and its clock would otherwise distort the whole
            day's wind, climb and timing statistics for everyone else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Flags */}
      <section id="flags" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Three further checks describe the flying rather than the file's identity. They are
            shown for a human to look at and <strong class="text-foreground">never affect a
            score</strong>, because each of them fires on flights that are entirely legitimate.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-muted-foreground">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Never left the take-off cylinder.</strong> Every
              fix stayed inside the declared take-off circle. That is exactly what a pilot who
              launches and sinks straight back down looks like — and such a pilot is correctly
              scored the minimum distance — but it is also what a recorder left running on
              launch looks like.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">No sign of flight.</strong> Nothing in the track
              resembles flying: no sustained climb (1.5 m/s held for two minutes) and no
              sustained glide (90 seconds descending at 0.8 m/s or more, never dropping below
              4 m/s over the ground, in a reasonably straight line). This is what catches a
              vario left recording the drive up to launch — a case speed alone cannot detect,
              since a retrieve vehicle easily outpaces a paraglider. A very short flight that
              never climbed can trip it too, which is why it only ever produces a note.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Implausible speed.</strong> The track covers
              ground faster than a glider sustains for a full minute — above about 119 km/h for
              paragliders or 162 km/h for hang gliders, tailwind included. The window is a
              minute precisely so GPS jitter and single bad fixes cannot trigger it. It is
              looking for something that isn't a glider at all: a flight home, a tow plane, two
              flights spliced into one file.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Score */}
      <section id="score" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What It Does to the Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Normally a pilot who launches and lands short is scored the
            <a href="/scoring/gap#minimum-distance" class="underline hover:text-foreground">minimum distance</a>
            — roughly 5 km — because the Sporting Code awards it to every pilot who takes off.
            That rule is unchanged, and these checks deliberately do not touch it: a short,
            unlucky flight still earns its minimum distance.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A withheld tracklog is different, because it is not evidence that the pilot flew
            this task at all. Such a pilot keeps their place in the results with
            <strong class="text-foreground">zero points</strong> and the reasons shown beside
            their name — they are never quietly dropped from the standings.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Withholding one track does not change anybody else's score. The pilot is left out
            of the launch-validity count (<SpecRef section="9.1" label="S7F §9.1" />) rather
            than counted as having flown or as present-but-not-flying, so an incorrect verdict
            can only ever cost that one pilot — it can never quietly reduce the whole field's
            points.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Override */}
      <section id="override" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Scorekeeper Decides</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Under
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">S7A §4.4.6</a>,
            rejecting a track log is the <strong class="text-foreground">organiser's</strong>
            judgement, not the software's. So an automatic verdict here is a default, never a
            final ruling: a scorekeeper can accept a flagged tracklog with one action, after
            which it is scored and analysed exactly like any other — its findings stay visible
            on the score page, and the decision is recorded in the competition's public audit
            log.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            If your track was withheld, the usual fix is simply to upload the right file for
            that task. If you believe the file <em>is</em> right, talk to the scorekeeper —
            they can overrule the check.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Calibration */}
      <section id="calibration" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How the Thresholds Were Chosen</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A check that wrongly zeroes a pilot is far worse than one that misses a bad file,
            so every threshold is set from real competition data rather than from intuition.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Across <strong class="text-foreground">4,762</strong> archived competition tracks,
            the typical flight passes within 70 m of a turnpoint, 99% pass within 1 km, and the
            furthest any honest track ever stayed from every turnpoint was 45 km — a flatlands
            pilot who landed a long way off course. The 100 km threshold sits at more than
            twice that worst case.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The one-day grace on the date check comes from the same evidence. One archived
            task has three pilots (of eighty) whose recorders were stamped a day late; they
            launched in the field's own window, flew the task, and were scored normally by the
            competition — one of them finished fourth, in goal. Treating a one-day offset as
            disqualifying would have zeroed a podium flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Run over every track GlideComp holds — <strong class="text-foreground">5,112</strong>
            flights across 186 tasks — the checks withhold exactly one: a New Zealand flight,
            from ten days after the task, submitted to a competition in Victoria.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Disclosure */}
      <section id="disclosure" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Full Disclosure on the Score Page</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Nothing here happens silently. Whenever a check fires, that pilot's score details
            page names it and explains it in full — which check, the actual numbers behind it,
            and what to do next. A withheld track is labelled as such in the results table too,
            so a score of zero is never left looking like a bug.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            This is the same transparency rule the rest of GlideComp follows — the one behind
            <a href="/scoring/data-cleaning" class="underline hover:text-foreground">track data cleaning</a>
            as well: every number that reaches a leaderboard must be explainable, and anything
            the system did on the way there must be visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
